1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:07,120
Hey! This is Text City Hall, for the CIVIC Hack challenge: making municipal decisions easier to understand.

2
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:18,800
Toronto council makes decisions every week that change our streets, but the record is buried in agendas, staff reports and votes, and most of us only find out once it's already decided!

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,200
Take Sam, who rents on Eglinton Avenue East and works shifts.

4
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:33,520
To learn what happened to the two 59-storey towers proposed on their street, Sam would need a thirty-page staff report, this agenda page, and votes from two different meetings.

5
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:36,000
Here's the same thing with Text City Hall.

6
00:00:36,213 --> 00:00:42,960
We're replaying the city's real records as they stood on July 8th, the day after North York Community Council met.

7
00:00:43,665 --> 00:00:46,545
Sam texts the address. No app, no account, any phone.

8
00:00:46,785 --> 00:00:51,665
It finds the ward in the city's own address data and lists the recent decisions there.

9
00:00:52,367 --> 00:00:53,247
Sam replies 1.

10
00:00:53,540 --> 00:01:01,487
On July 7th, community council passed an amendment six to nothing, but the motion to adopt the item lost, two to four.

11
00:01:01,727 --> 00:01:06,847
No comment period is confirmed, so it says so. It never guesses a deadline from a meeting date.

12
00:01:06,954 --> 00:01:09,327
Every reply links to the official record.

13
00:01:10,028 --> 00:01:11,388
Sam texts WATCH.

14
00:01:11,761 --> 00:01:23,068
On July 30th, City Council votes, and Sam gets two texts: Eglinton, amended twenty-five to one, and a Millwood Road rezoning in the same ward, amended twelve to eleven.

15
00:01:23,769 --> 00:01:28,969
Reply 1 again, and Sam sees the outcome: City Council adopted it, with amendments.

16
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:41,350
Everything you just saw runs on City of Toronto open data and agenda pages. No AI writes the replies, and on two thousand real addresses the ward lookup was never wrong.

17
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:45,510
Setting it up is simple. There's no server and no database to run.

18
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:57,270
A nightly script refreshes the city's open data, the site is free to host, and a city connects a text-message number to the same reply code, so residents get exactly this conversation.

19
00:01:57,403 --> 00:01:59,590
It costs a few cents per conversation.

20
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:05,510
Any city that publishes council votes and addresses can adapt it by changing one data step.

21
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:13,110
Council is between terms after the election, so this demo replays real records from July, clearly labelled.

22
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:17,110
The goal: residents hear about decisions while they can still have a say.

23
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:22,870
Try it at ripasso.github.io/civichacks.
